# Classify the native plan before applying it

The Alchemy plan is the authority for proposed changes. The deploy adapter owns its in-memory snapshot. Rows and receipts are derived views.

## Policy

- Read the native plan, not terminal formatting.
- Include resource, binding and task changes.
- Pass only updates and noops without an exact allow entry.
- Require separate owner sign-off for deletion, replacement, orphaning and unbinding.
- Apply the same native plan under its original session.
- Keep the XState lifecycle responsible for step order and terminal outcomes.
- Send Alchemy confirmations through the capability approval service. Other unattended prompts fail typed.

Retention protects physical objects, not state rows. Alchemy removes retained objects from state without deleting their cloud objects. The receipt lists confirmed retained orphans. A retention change appears as a noop and reaches state only on apply.

## Build and live evidence

Deploy commands build every workspace output before planning. The prepared receipt records the expected content generation before apply. Apply failures and crashes retain that intent.

Content checks compare the built asset generation with live root and agent-text ETags. They request identity encoding and add a cache-busting query. They accept strong and weak ETags for the same generation. A missing header gives unknown, not pass. Evidence records the exact header.

The readiness check makes at most two attempts. It records each outcome, duration, scan time and returned cache flags. A scan from before the apply cannot prove readiness. A scanner-reported site error remains red. A later fresh pass can settle the check without discarding the first failure.

The apply receipt also records prior live Worker version IDs before mutation. They support an owner-approved recovery; no rollback command is implemented.

## Installed API boundary

Checked against Alchemy 2.0.0-beta.80 and installed Effect 4.0.0 source.

`Deploy.ts` runs `evalStack`, `Plan.make` and `Apply.apply`. The exported Alchemist stack route preserves the native plan and session between calls. The adapter uses that route because it also loads the entrypoint, selects the profile and refuses state-store bootstrap.

Internal imports retained in `packages/deploy/src/local.ts`:

| Import | Reason |
| --- | --- |
| `alchemy/Alchemist/routes/stack` | Keep the exact native plan and its session between machine steps. |
| `alchemy/Alchemist/Session` | Load the entrypoint's auth-provider registry without applying the Stack. |
| `alchemy/Auth/AuthProvider` | Resolve the selected provider; the root exports the registry but not its lookup function. |
| `alchemy/Auth/Credentials` | Provide Alchemy's credential-store implementation. |
| `alchemy/Auth/Profile` | Read and refresh the named profile using Alchemy's store. |
| `alchemy/State/State` | Read persisted Worker attributes for version evidence. |

Other imported Alchemy modules expose their services through exported subpaths. `Plan` and `AuthProviders` use root exports.

## Proof seams

- `apps/infra/test/retention.test.ts` compiles the real Stack with capture both on and off. It enumerates durable declarations without provider calls.
- `packages/deploy/test/plan.test.ts` generates native-plan policy shapes. It tests exact exceptions and separate owner sign-off.
- `packages/deploy/test/machine.model.test.ts` checks generated step histories and preserves prepared intent on an apply crash.
- `packages/deploy/test/interaction.test.ts` checks the approval boundary and typed unattended refusal.
- `packages/deploy/test/version-readiness.test.ts` checks stale, missing and fresh version evidence and bounded scan histories.

Removing bucket retention makes the Stack property fail. Removing owner-sign-off enforcement makes two plan properties fail. Preview lifecycle, expiry and CI remain separate work.
